{#def dismiss, backdrop_id="", max_width="max-w-lg", card_extra="" #}
{#
  The centered dialog every overlay-hosted modal page draws: a dimming
  full-window backdrop, a card centered on it, and the close X. Sign-in,
  Accounts, a plan, Minds Settings, sharing and the workspace options panel
  all had their own copy of this shape, which is how they drifted apart on
  padding and corner radius.

  The card's surface, padding (p-8) and radius are FIXED here on purpose --
  they are what make an overlay dialog look like one, and a caller that wants
  its own is the drift this component exists to stop. What legitimately varies
  is size, so that is what the props cover.

  ``dismiss`` is a JS expression, not a function name: each page defines its
  own dismisser (the shell's closeModal in Electron, a navigation in browser
  mode). It runs on the X and on a backdrop click -- the target check keeps a
  click INSIDE the card from dismissing it, which every page previously wired
  by hand in its own inline script.

  ``max_width`` is a Tailwind width utility (``max-w-lg`` … ``max-w-4xl``, or
  an arbitrary ``max-w-[880px]``). ``card_extra`` carries the height/layout
  the page needs: a fixed ``h-[85vh] flex flex-col`` for one that pins a
  header and scrolls its body, ``max-h-[85vh] overflow-y-auto`` for one that
  simply grows to fit, or nothing at all for a short dialog.

  Any other attribute on the tag (a ``data-*`` the page's script reads off the
  backdrop, say) flows through to the backdrop element via JinjaX's ``attrs``
  passthrough.

  Drawers and menus on this surface (the inbox, the workspace switcher) are
  NOT dialogs and do not belong here -- they are edge-anchored, sized to their
  content, and have their own chrome.
#}
<div {% if backdrop_id %}id="{{ backdrop_id }}"{% endif %} {{ attrs.render() }}
     onclick="if (event.target === this) { {{ dismiss }} }"
     class="fixed inset-0 flex items-start justify-center overflow-y-auto bg-surface-overlay p-4">
  {# items-start + overflow-y-auto + my-auto, not items-center: a card taller
     than the window (the sign-up form on a short screen) is centered while it
     fits and scrolls once it does not. Centering alone clipped it off both
     ends with no way to reach either. #}
  <div class="relative my-auto bg-surface-primary rounded-lg shadow-overlay {{ max_width }} w-full p-8 text-left {{ card_extra }}">
    <DialogCloseButton :onclick="dismiss" />
    {{ content }}
  </div>
</div>
